WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.120
You know, that moment when something just clicks, like maybe it's some gears in a kid's

00:05.120 --> 00:06.280
toy, right?

00:06.380 --> 00:09.060
Or that really complex spreadsheet you've been staring at.

00:09.180 --> 00:09.360
Yeah.

00:09.480 --> 00:10.620
Suddenly it makes sense.

00:11.000 --> 00:15.600
There's this real satisfaction, you know, definitely that feeling of understanding how

00:15.600 --> 00:16.260
things work.

00:16.440 --> 00:16.920
Exactly.

00:17.220 --> 00:21.340
And what if, I mean, what if those moments, the toy in the spreadsheet, what if they're

00:21.340 --> 00:24.520
actually like connected, part of the same thing?

00:24.640 --> 00:26.640
It's a really compelling idea, isn't it?

00:26.640 --> 00:31.460
That playing with a simple doll maybe isn't so different fundamentally from figuring out

00:31.460 --> 00:32.280
a strategy game.

00:32.420 --> 00:35.360
That they both tell us something about how we grasp the world.

00:35.640 --> 00:35.740
Right.

00:36.040 --> 00:41.620
We've been digging into this fascinating paper that introduces this concept called the play

00:41.620 --> 00:42.000
cosm.

00:42.300 --> 00:43.180
The play cosm.

00:43.340 --> 00:43.500
Yeah.

00:43.620 --> 00:48.980
Think of it as this like unified space, an arena where all the ways we play, physical

00:48.980 --> 00:52.840
stuff, digital stuff, it all serves as a kind of simulation.

00:53.000 --> 00:54.220
Simulations of what exactly?

00:54.220 --> 00:58.040
Of the real world systems, the sort of institutional ecosystems that shape our lives.

00:58.360 --> 00:58.500
Okay.

00:58.660 --> 01:04.620
So the way kid plays with dolls, arranging them, that's not just, you know, fun.

01:04.860 --> 01:07.380
It's actually a way of understanding social structures.

01:07.660 --> 01:08.280
That's the idea.

01:08.420 --> 01:11.760
Kind of like how a gamer figures out alliances in a game.

01:11.880 --> 01:12.940
That's the core argument.

01:13.080 --> 01:13.280
Yeah.

01:13.700 --> 01:17.520
So for this deep dive, we really want to unpack this play cosm framework.

01:17.520 --> 01:23.500
We're going to explore how things that seem totally separate, like customizing a Barbie

01:23.500 --> 01:29.780
or pushing a toy car or even managing a city in Age of Empires, how they're all just different

01:29.780 --> 01:32.720
ways we simulate how our world works.

01:33.240 --> 01:38.260
And the research makes a pretty strong case suggesting that through play, our brains are

01:38.260 --> 01:42.600
actively training themselves, learning to predict how these complex systems behave.

01:42.740 --> 01:43.000
Right.

01:43.000 --> 01:45.660
And it brings up a really interesting point about, you know, gamification.

01:45.980 --> 01:46.640
Ah, yes.

01:46.740 --> 01:47.700
The points and badges.

01:47.900 --> 01:48.180
Exactly.

01:48.340 --> 01:50.000
All those reward systems you see everywhere.

01:50.400 --> 01:55.140
The paper argues they often miss the deeper potential, the real power of genuine play.

01:55.460 --> 02:00.860
And there's also this idea that toys, you know, simple things, can actually be seen as early

02:00.860 --> 02:03.400
versions, maybe prototypes of future technologies.

02:03.820 --> 02:04.200
Absolutely.

02:04.560 --> 02:08.040
And it also touches on something called privilege gates within these simulations.

02:08.280 --> 02:09.080
Privilege gates.

02:09.560 --> 02:10.260
Sounds intriguing.

02:10.260 --> 02:16.540
Yeah, basically how our access, our role within these play worlds affects what we experience

02:16.540 --> 02:18.720
and ultimately what we understand.

02:19.040 --> 02:19.160
Okay.

02:19.240 --> 02:23.800
So our mission today is to really get to grips with this play cosm framework.

02:24.140 --> 02:29.660
See what it tells us about learning, about technology and, well, about how we make sense of the world.

02:29.800 --> 02:29.980
Right.

02:30.080 --> 02:31.420
Let's start with this core idea.

02:31.980 --> 02:34.420
The play cosm as a single shared universe.

02:34.420 --> 02:34.760
Okay.

02:34.880 --> 02:40.560
So this single shard idea, it means it's not just like a bunch of separate games or toys.

02:40.740 --> 02:41.240
No, exactly.

02:41.540 --> 02:43.760
It's presented as this interconnected universe.

02:44.300 --> 02:48.120
Imagine one huge ongoing game world, but it includes everything.

02:48.260 --> 02:51.720
The old toys in the attic, the latest VR experience, all of it.

02:51.720 --> 02:54.240
And the key is seeing all these different forms of play.

02:54.360 --> 02:59.040
Dolls, cars, Age of Empires, The Sims, seeing them all as simulations.

02:59.340 --> 03:00.580
Yes, they're all simulations.

03:00.900 --> 03:02.640
What makes them different are the affordances.

03:03.120 --> 03:05.100
Affordances, meaning what you can do in each one.

03:05.200 --> 03:05.560
Pretty much.

03:05.920 --> 03:08.000
The range of actions and interactions they allow.

03:08.360 --> 03:08.620
Okay.

03:08.860 --> 03:11.620
So a toy car lets you, what does the paper say?

03:12.800 --> 03:13.760
Simulate journey.

03:13.900 --> 03:14.100
Yeah.

03:14.180 --> 03:15.760
Within a mobility ecosystem.

03:15.760 --> 03:20.060
And a Barbie lets you enact role within a social hierarchy.

03:20.420 --> 03:21.200
An Age of Empires.

03:21.340 --> 03:23.860
You're running managed resources within a city ecosystem.

03:24.100 --> 03:24.380
Exactly.

03:24.540 --> 03:28.880
And the paper breaks down the kinds of institutions that sort of run this play cosm behind the scenes.

03:29.860 --> 03:30.640
Institutions like?

03:30.900 --> 03:34.180
Well, like factories that produce the stuff, the toys, the game assets.

03:34.400 --> 03:39.320
Then farms, which are more about cultivating behaviors like the rules of play, game rules.

03:39.500 --> 03:39.660
Okay.

03:39.860 --> 03:41.920
Factories for objects, farms for behaviors.

03:42.300 --> 03:44.740
And then ecosystems are where the feedback happens.

03:44.740 --> 03:47.680
You know, your actions have consequences in the game.

03:47.800 --> 03:50.140
Or how playing inspires new ideas.

03:50.400 --> 03:50.460
Right.

03:50.560 --> 03:51.340
The cause and effect.

03:51.620 --> 03:55.120
And finally, this idea of object-oriented programs, OPS.

03:55.560 --> 03:58.400
They're like the underlying code managing the complexity.

03:58.700 --> 04:01.940
Like style doll dictating how you change a doll's look.

04:02.180 --> 04:02.800
Ah, okay.

04:02.880 --> 04:08.500
So these institutions, factories, farms, ecosystems, OOPs, they shape the affordances.

04:08.940 --> 04:09.420
Directly.

04:09.680 --> 04:11.840
The design of the toy car dictates its movement.

04:12.320 --> 04:14.320
The rules of monopoly define what you can do.

04:14.320 --> 04:16.140
The game code sets the boundaries.

04:16.840 --> 04:21.980
So pushing a toy car and managing a virtual city, they feel completely different.

04:22.200 --> 04:23.300
Totally different experiences.

04:23.500 --> 04:25.460
But fundamentally, they're both training us.

04:25.660 --> 04:29.220
Training us to model these institutional ecosystems.

04:29.660 --> 04:30.380
That's the argument.

04:30.800 --> 04:30.940
Yeah.

04:31.060 --> 04:33.960
Unified forms of play, just with different affordances.

04:34.140 --> 04:36.900
But crucially, the experience isn't the same for everyone, right?

04:36.960 --> 04:38.500
That's where these privilege gates come in.

04:38.600 --> 04:39.340
Yes, exactly.

04:39.800 --> 04:40.420
Privilege gates.

04:40.420 --> 04:42.820
It does sound a bit, well, gatekeep-y, doesn't it?

04:42.940 --> 04:43.580
Ah, yeah.

04:44.040 --> 04:48.280
But it's a useful way to think about how access works in these simulations.

04:48.600 --> 04:49.740
So what are they technically?

04:49.940 --> 04:51.880
They're basically the institutional mechanisms.

04:52.020 --> 04:55.660
Things like standards, rules, how things are made and sold.

04:56.200 --> 04:58.020
Mechanisms that limit your affordances.

04:58.400 --> 04:59.460
What you can do or see.

04:59.460 --> 05:04.260
Based on things like money, status, knowledge.

05:04.420 --> 05:05.080
Yeah, all of those.

05:05.240 --> 05:08.860
Wealth, status, knowledge, even the role you're assigned in a game.

05:08.980 --> 05:11.420
The paper uses that programming analogy, right?

05:11.580 --> 05:12.480
Access modifiers.

05:13.040 --> 05:13.380
Exactly.

05:13.700 --> 05:17.440
Like private or protected in code, shielding certain functions.

05:17.820 --> 05:23.680
So in the Playcosm, maybe a high-privileged player gets access to design road.

05:23.820 --> 05:25.460
They can actually shape the infrastructure.

05:25.700 --> 05:28.620
Whereas a low-privileged player can only navigate road.

05:28.620 --> 05:31.020
They use the road, but can't build it.

05:31.180 --> 05:31.280
Right.

05:31.580 --> 05:33.100
Or think about Barbie again.

05:33.500 --> 05:35.900
The fanciest outfits might cost more real money.

05:36.520 --> 05:37.740
Currency becomes the gate.

05:37.960 --> 05:42.040
Or in a strategy game, maybe the best tech is locked behind a subscription.

05:42.440 --> 05:43.460
That's a privilege gate.

05:43.720 --> 05:47.360
And the result is what the paper calls stratified simulations.

05:48.040 --> 05:50.480
Stratified, meaning layered, unequal.

05:50.700 --> 05:50.900
Yeah.

05:51.600 --> 05:54.440
Different players experience different slices of the whole ecosystem.

05:55.020 --> 05:57.780
High privilege often means seeing and doing more.

05:57.780 --> 06:00.640
And it's not just actions, but visibility, too.

06:00.760 --> 06:00.940
Right.

06:01.120 --> 06:05.560
High-privileged actions, like someone building a whole city in a game, might only be visible

06:05.560 --> 06:08.280
to others through the effects, like, oh, there's new trade now.

06:08.620 --> 06:10.240
But you don't see the decisions behind it.

06:10.340 --> 06:10.660
Wow.

06:10.780 --> 06:11.020
Okay.

06:11.320 --> 06:14.120
That really does echo real-world power dynamics.

06:14.220 --> 06:14.880
It does, doesn't it?

06:15.240 --> 06:19.980
These gates mean that even though it's a shared Playcosm, the playing field is definitely not

06:19.980 --> 06:20.280
level.

06:20.540 --> 06:20.800
Okay.

06:20.800 --> 06:22.580
So we have this unified Playcosm.

06:22.680 --> 06:24.060
We have these privilege gates.

06:24.800 --> 06:25.520
But why?

06:25.860 --> 06:27.120
What's the point of all this play?

06:27.200 --> 06:29.620
How does it actually, you know, help us understand things?

06:29.780 --> 06:29.840
Right.

06:29.940 --> 06:30.860
The connection to learning.

06:31.200 --> 06:33.240
This is where it links up with cognitive science.

06:33.380 --> 06:33.500
Yeah.

06:33.920 --> 06:35.980
Specifically the idea of predictive processing.

06:36.220 --> 06:37.200
Predictive processing.

06:37.420 --> 06:37.620
Yeah.

06:37.620 --> 06:40.240
Our brain's trying to guess what happens next.

06:40.580 --> 06:41.520
Essentially, yes.

06:42.080 --> 06:44.920
We build internal models of the world to anticipate outcomes.

06:45.520 --> 06:50.400
And play, according to this framework, is the main way we build and test those models

06:50.400 --> 06:51.520
within the Playcosm.

06:51.660 --> 06:53.880
So pushing a toy car isn't just moving plastic.

06:54.560 --> 06:57.800
It's building a little mental model of physics, of movement.

06:57.920 --> 06:58.440
Precisely.

06:58.880 --> 07:03.160
Or dressing up a doll is simulating social roles, understanding hierarchies.

07:03.600 --> 07:06.560
Managing resources in civilization is modeling governance.

07:06.560 --> 07:08.640
We're constantly running these little mental experiments.

07:08.840 --> 07:08.880
Okay.

07:08.960 --> 07:09.200
I see.

07:09.480 --> 07:11.140
The unifying thing is that function.

07:11.260 --> 07:11.460
Yeah.

07:11.620 --> 07:13.620
Modeling these institutional ecosystems.

07:13.980 --> 07:14.420
Exactly.

07:15.020 --> 07:19.420
The affordances differ tactile feedback versus complex strategy, but the core purpose is

07:19.420 --> 07:19.840
the same.

07:20.160 --> 07:21.340
I like that analogy in the paper.

07:21.720 --> 07:24.300
A toy car is like Age of Empires with touch feedback.

07:25.060 --> 07:27.120
Barbie is like The Sims with social cues.

07:27.300 --> 07:27.620
Yeah.

07:27.880 --> 07:29.500
It helps bridge that gap, doesn't it?

07:29.520 --> 07:31.260
It shows how there are variations on a theme.

07:31.260 --> 07:36.320
So fundamentally, all play trains us to predict how rule-bound systems work.

07:36.320 --> 07:40.280
Whether it's physics for building blocks or social rules and make-believe, yeah.

07:40.700 --> 07:43.580
We learn to anticipate based on the rules of that simulation.

07:43.900 --> 07:46.940
It's like rehearsal for the real world, but in a safer space.

07:47.040 --> 07:47.820
Kind of, yeah.

07:48.180 --> 07:53.040
Now, the paper also talks about play having its own language, a semiotic grammar.

07:53.620 --> 07:54.300
What's that about?

07:54.540 --> 07:56.100
Yeah, this is a really neat part of it.

07:56.160 --> 07:57.880
It's saying play isn't just doing stuff.

07:57.940 --> 07:58.960
It's about making meaning.

07:59.760 --> 08:02.000
An embodied semiotic process.

08:02.300 --> 08:03.340
Embodied semiotics.

08:03.340 --> 08:03.700
Okay.

08:04.100 --> 08:06.440
Meaning the objects and actions are the language.

08:06.680 --> 08:07.560
They teach us stuff.

08:07.900 --> 08:11.080
So pushing the car, the action itself means something.

08:11.180 --> 08:11.340
Right.

08:11.500 --> 08:16.100
It encodes move travel, swapping Barbie outfits encodes style role.

08:16.360 --> 08:16.520
Yeah.

08:16.600 --> 08:18.940
Clicking to build encodes command, grow.

08:19.380 --> 08:21.360
Ah, like a visual interactive grammar.

08:21.680 --> 08:22.120
Exactly.

08:22.280 --> 08:23.360
A simulation grammar.

08:23.940 --> 08:27.800
And we learn it through doing, often without consciously realizing it.

08:28.200 --> 08:32.660
It ties into tacit knowledge, getting good at the simulation before you can explain the rules.

08:32.940 --> 08:38.360
And the institutions, the toy makers, the game devs, they're the ones shaping this language.

08:38.840 --> 08:40.960
Setting the standards for the signs we learn.

08:41.180 --> 08:41.820
Pretty much.

08:41.820 --> 08:44.260
The design of the toy, the rules of the game.

08:44.740 --> 08:46.920
They create the affordances, the signs.

08:47.540 --> 08:50.400
A realistic toy car teaches about real cars.

08:51.020 --> 08:54.340
A doll embodying certain ideals teaches those ideals.

08:54.600 --> 08:56.800
And the privilege gates affect this language learning too.

08:57.020 --> 08:57.340
Definitely.

08:57.920 --> 09:01.120
More accessories for the doll means a richer vocabulary about status.

09:01.540 --> 09:04.600
Limited options means a more limited understanding of that simulation.

09:05.100 --> 09:09.620
The factories create the language, but the gates control who learns which dialects, you could say.

09:09.720 --> 09:10.800
This is fascinating.

09:10.800 --> 09:13.940
But it makes me wonder about, well, gamification again.

09:14.340 --> 09:15.680
Those workplace point systems.

09:15.980 --> 09:18.000
How does this deep simulation compare to that?

09:18.300 --> 09:20.220
The paper calls it shallow gamification.

09:20.700 --> 09:22.660
Yeah, that's a really important contrast.

09:23.120 --> 09:30.960
The argument is that most gamification points, badges, leaderboards, just copies the surface appearance of games.

09:31.080 --> 09:33.600
It misses the generative power.

09:33.840 --> 09:34.260
Generative power.

09:34.500 --> 09:38.120
Yeah, the stuff that makes real play engaging and useful for learning.

09:38.120 --> 09:41.480
These shallow systems usually lack those key affordances.

09:41.860 --> 09:43.120
Okay, what affordances are missing?

09:43.120 --> 09:45.180
The paper points out three main things.

09:45.520 --> 09:47.500
First, platform expansion.

09:47.800 --> 09:48.060
Meaning?

09:48.300 --> 09:51.460
Opportunities to grow your role, expand what you can do as you get better.

09:51.900 --> 09:56.100
Second, emergent goal structures where new goals arise from playing.

09:56.360 --> 10:01.560
And third, strategic ambiguity where the rules aren't totally fixed, allowing exploration.

10:01.940 --> 10:03.900
And shallow systems lack these.

10:04.020 --> 10:04.560
Often, yeah.

10:04.800 --> 10:06.620
The paper calls them non-expanding shards.

10:06.900 --> 10:07.460
They're static.

10:07.640 --> 10:09.460
They don't really let you grow or change things.

10:09.560 --> 10:10.220
Can you give an example?

10:10.220 --> 10:12.300
Like, the lack of platform expansion.

10:12.540 --> 10:12.820
Sure.

10:13.200 --> 10:16.980
In a good game, leveling up might unlock totally new ways to play.

10:17.460 --> 10:20.100
In Gamified Office, you get a badge for being on time.

10:20.400 --> 10:23.860
But you probably can't use that badge to, say, redesign the workflow.

10:24.120 --> 10:27.140
You don't get access to defined protocol or architect workflow.

10:27.500 --> 10:27.700
Right.

10:27.760 --> 10:29.000
It's just a sticker for doing your job.

10:29.340 --> 10:30.320
What about static metrics?

10:30.760 --> 10:32.280
That's the Goodhart's Law problem.

10:32.940 --> 10:35.660
When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.

10:35.660 --> 10:41.100
People just focus on hitting the metric, like call volume, even if it hurts the actual goal,

10:41.300 --> 10:42.520
like customer satisfaction.

10:43.080 --> 10:46.380
Good games have dynamic feedback to stop simple exploits.

10:46.540 --> 10:48.620
And no meta-level renegotiation.

10:48.840 --> 10:51.480
In deep play, people invent their own challenges, right?

10:51.740 --> 10:53.840
Speed runs, mods, you can change the game.

10:54.420 --> 10:57.600
Shallow systems, usually fixed rules, fixed roles.

10:57.880 --> 11:01.640
No way for users to tweak the system or create their own metagame.

11:01.640 --> 11:07.120
So shallow gamification is just simulating control, not giving real agency,

11:07.440 --> 11:09.840
not real sovereignty, as the paper puts it.

11:10.080 --> 11:10.840
That's the argument.

11:11.020 --> 11:13.940
It uses extrinsic rewards, but doesn't foster that deep,

11:14.100 --> 11:16.140
intrinsic learning or understanding of the system.

11:16.240 --> 11:17.720
It kind of flattens the whole experience.

11:17.900 --> 11:18.040
Okay.

11:18.520 --> 11:22.460
Now, shifting gears slightly, this idea that toys can be prototypes,

11:22.700 --> 11:26.100
that new tech often shows up in the playcosm first, that's wild.

11:26.240 --> 11:27.500
It is pretty mind-bending, yeah.

11:27.680 --> 11:29.760
The idea is that advancements are prefigured.

11:29.760 --> 11:34.680
They exist as simulations in toys, sci-fi, play long before they're actually built.

11:35.020 --> 11:37.420
So a kid with a toy drone isn't just mimicking.

11:38.440 --> 11:41.160
They're kind of beta-testing the concept.

11:41.660 --> 11:42.680
In a way, yes.

11:43.120 --> 11:47.020
These proto-artifacts, as the paper calls them, evolve through play.

11:47.600 --> 11:52.100
They shape how we think about future tech, our sociotechnical imaginaries.

11:52.320 --> 11:55.680
Sociotechnical imaginaries, our shared ideas about future tech.

11:55.760 --> 11:56.180
Exactly.

11:56.180 --> 11:59.400
The playcosm acts as an epistemic incubator.

11:59.760 --> 12:02.340
A place to rehearse future tech, symbolically.

12:02.640 --> 12:03.980
Can you give historical examples?

12:04.280 --> 12:05.720
Well, think about wheeled toys.

12:06.300 --> 12:09.040
They existed long before complex roads and vehicles,

12:09.420 --> 12:12.420
but playing with them helped build a basic understanding of wheels,

12:12.880 --> 12:16.980
movement, friction, or model planes and gliders before powered flight,

12:17.360 --> 12:19.700
exploring aerodynamics in a low-stakes way.

12:19.700 --> 12:22.520
The paper calls these precompilable affordances, right?

12:22.680 --> 12:25.840
Like functions we can simulate mentally even if the tech isn't there yet?

12:25.940 --> 12:26.280
Exactly.

12:26.480 --> 12:28.500
We develop procedural fluency through play.

12:28.580 --> 12:31.360
We're kind of debugging the future in the playcosm before it arrives.

12:31.440 --> 12:33.200
So the playcosm is like a forecasting engine.

12:33.400 --> 12:33.920
In a sense.

12:34.300 --> 12:36.400
The toy logic, the rules of these simulations,

12:36.620 --> 12:38.580
lets us test out imagined futures.

12:39.100 --> 12:41.440
The future often plays itself into being.

12:41.800 --> 12:42.080
Wow.

12:42.080 --> 12:44.820
Okay, so play isn't just about the present,

12:45.140 --> 12:48.260
but also about envisioning the future.

12:48.580 --> 12:51.680
So how do we actually get better at these semi-contents?

12:51.760 --> 12:52.420
What about feedback?

12:52.740 --> 12:53.760
Feedback is crucial.

12:54.180 --> 12:55.160
Play is iterative.

12:55.400 --> 12:56.740
The toy car hits a wall.

12:56.920 --> 12:57.360
Feedback.

12:57.520 --> 12:58.380
Learn about obstacles.

12:58.600 --> 12:58.800
Right.

12:59.260 --> 13:02.320
A play scenario feels engaging positive feedback.

13:02.940 --> 13:04.840
Losing a game feedback on strategy.

13:05.200 --> 13:07.620
And these loops connect back to the playcosm ecosystem.

13:08.160 --> 13:09.820
Player actions influence the factories.

13:09.820 --> 13:13.080
Yeah, toy companies or game devs see what people like,

13:13.180 --> 13:14.780
what works, and they adapt.

13:14.960 --> 13:16.120
They change the affordances,

13:16.340 --> 13:18.160
release new versions based on that feedback.

13:18.440 --> 13:20.480
But the privilege gates affect feedback too.

13:20.760 --> 13:21.160
Absolutely.

13:21.620 --> 13:23.720
If you have access to more complex simulations,

13:23.980 --> 13:24.900
you get richer feedback.

13:25.620 --> 13:27.500
Modifying game rules gives different insights

13:27.500 --> 13:28.540
than just following them.

13:28.620 --> 13:31.040
And what happens if we stop playing?

13:31.780 --> 13:32.540
If we disengage?

13:32.980 --> 13:35.180
The paper warns about homebound cognition.

13:35.360 --> 13:36.120
Homebound cognition.

13:36.240 --> 13:39.300
Yeah, like if we avoid certain types of play or tools,

13:39.300 --> 13:40.680
saying that's not for me,

13:40.960 --> 13:43.080
our internal simulations can get rigid,

13:43.360 --> 13:43.860
out of date,

13:44.040 --> 13:45.720
unfit for the real dynamic world,

13:45.840 --> 13:48.120
like being stuck in a game's tutorial level forever.

13:48.460 --> 13:48.760
Oof.

