13:55.000 --> 13:56.200
So taking all this,

13:56.560 --> 13:57.840
the playcosm, the gates,

13:57.980 --> 13:59.440
the feedback, prefiguration,

13:59.820 --> 14:03.340
what does it mean for designing things in the real world?

14:03.820 --> 14:04.980
Like institutions.

14:04.980 --> 14:09.880
The paper argues we should see institutional design as simulation engineering.

14:10.540 --> 14:14.680
We need to consciously think about privilege, play, and prefiguration

14:14.680 --> 14:19.100
when we build anything from an app interface to social policy.

14:19.100 --> 14:24.520
So designing the affordances deliberately to help people build good mental models.

14:24.520 --> 14:25.020
Exactly.

14:25.280 --> 14:27.780
A good tool should feel intuitive, like a good toy.

14:28.260 --> 14:30.700
An organization's rules should be clear, like game rules.

14:31.080 --> 14:35.860
And we need to be really mindful of those privilege gates in the real world balanced complexity with access.

14:35.980 --> 14:37.640
And avoid that shallow gamification trap.

14:37.780 --> 14:38.100
Definitely.

14:38.360 --> 14:41.260
Focus on systems that allow for real growth, emergent goals,

14:41.580 --> 14:44.960
maybe even letting users adapt the rules, foster genuine play.

14:45.080 --> 14:48.660
And encourage prefigurative toys, things that let us simulate future possibilities.

14:48.960 --> 14:49.360
Absolutely.

14:49.740 --> 14:53.180
Tools, platforms for experimenting with the future in low-risk ways.

14:53.180 --> 14:57.960
There's that universal principle mentioned too, that everything should help build simulations.

14:58.520 --> 15:02.760
Yeah, the idea that whether it's a road sign, a tool handle, or a government form,

15:03.060 --> 15:07.060
it should be designed to be understandable, to help you simulate how it works,

15:07.080 --> 15:09.860
like a toy car, a doll, a game interface.

15:10.020 --> 15:14.640
So if we integrate open play, balanced gates, prefigurative design,

15:15.080 --> 15:19.240
we get a better playcosm, both in literal play and in the world.

15:19.240 --> 15:24.260
That's the hope. A playcosm where more people can understand and actually shape the systems around them.

15:24.400 --> 15:28.800
